e being dyslexic  because first of all I mean to some  extent in English you can't necessarily  tell how a word is pronounced just by  the way it's spelled but that's much  more of a problem in Arabic where in  many of the texts of owls are not  indicated so you just have consonants  and you don't know they're it's an O or  an e in between the consonants and plus  I'm still getting used to these letters  and I still confuse some of them and so  I might be like the dyslexic reader but  I am able to read by looking words up by  clicking on the text to speech and then  hearing the natural voice and slowly  slowly slowly this improves my ability  to read to the point where now I can  pick up a paper book which I did for  this I had bought this harems method  Express for learning Arabic and the font  is so small I couldn't read it to save  my life but now I can read it and I  think that the same is true could be  true for people who suffer from dyslexia  at any rate I would like to find out one  day if what we do at link and the  functionality there would be helpful to  people who are dyslexic or people who  have trouble with literacy but in any  case reading is probably the most  efficient least cost way of acquiring  information and and I should say too  that you know reading is like is like  listening without the audio because very  often especially if it's a foreign  language we are sub vocalizing as we are  reading and conversely when I hear a  word I the image that I have is the  written word so if it were English and I  and I think of the word read I don't see  the color red  see re D and when I listen to Arabic now  more and more I am able to visualize the  written word that corresponds to that  spoken word if I know the word if I  don't know the word I can't do that so I  have that kind of a relationship to Arab  words I have to know them I can't  necessarily read an unknown word and  pronounce it correctly all by myself but  I am able to connect the sounds of  